%[text] # Coffee Parameterized — Exploring the Environment
%[text] ## Scenario
%[text] In the original **coffee** exercise the environmental constants, heat-transfer coefficient $k$ and ambient temperature $\\theta\_e$ , were *hard-coded inside* the rate function. To explore a different room or a different cup we would have had to edit and re-save the rate function each time. 
%[text] In this assignment we **parameterize** the rate function so the same MATLAB code can be reused across a sweep of environments. The technique is called pairing a parameterized function with an **anonymous-function wrapper**
%%
%[text] ## The Model 
%[text] Newton's Law of Cooling:
%[text] $$$\\frac{dy}{dt} = -k\\,(y - \\theta\_e)$
%[text] with
%[text] - $y(t)$ — coffee temperature (°C) at time $t$ (min)
%[text] - $k$ — heat-transfer coefficient (min⁻¹)
%[text] - $\\theta\_e$ — ambient (room) temperature (°C) \
function dydt = coffee_rate(t, y)
    k       = 0.01;   % heat-transfer coefficient (min^-1)
    theta_e = 20;     % room temperature (deg C)
    dydt    = -k * (y - theta_e);
end
%[text] Initial condition: $y(0) = 90$ °C . 
y0 = 90;          % initial coffee temperature (deg C)
%[text] Verify with the initial condition — we should still get −0.70 °C/min.
rate_orig = coffee_rate(0, y0)
%%
%[text] ## Parameterized Rate Function
%[text] Add a **new** local function whose signature carries the environment as arguments:
%[text] $$ $\\texttt{dydt = coffee\\\_rate\\\_parameterized}(t,\\,y,\\,k,\\,\\theta\_e)$
%[text] The body has the same math; only the source of `k` and `theta_e` changes. 
function dydt = coffee_rate_parameterized(t, y, k, theta_e)
    % YOUR CODE
end
%[text] Verify that calling the parameterized form with the original constants reproduces the original rate function:
rate_param = coffee_rate_parameterized(0, y0, 0.01, 20)
%%
%[text] ## Wrap with an Anonymous Function
%[text] `ode45` expects a rate function with the signature `f(t, y)` , with exactly two inputs. Our parameterized version has fourThe fix is an **anonymous-function wrapper** that includes `k` and `theta_e` from the workspace and exposes the `(t, y)` interface that `ode45` requires
%[text] Use the same parameter values as before so we know what to expect in the solution.
k       = 0.01;   % heat-transfer coefficient (min^-1)
theta_e = 20;     % ambient temperature (deg C)

rate_func_handle = @(t, y) coffee_rate_parameterized(% YOUR CODE);
%[text] At creation time, MATLAB captures the current values of `k` and `theta_e` inside the handle. Even if we change those variables later, this particular handle still uses 0.01 and 20. 
%[text] Solve and plot — the result should overlay the original `coffee_rate` solution exactly.
tspan = [0, 600];
[t_check, y_check] = % YOUR CODE
[t_orig,  y_orig ] = % YOUR CODE
figure(1); clf
plot(t_orig, y_orig, 'b-',  'LineWidth', 2.5); hold on
plot(t_check, y_check, 'r--', 'LineWidth', 1.5)
xlabel('Time (min)'); ylabel('Temperature (°C)')
title('Verification: parameterized handle vs. original')
legend('original coffee\_rate', 'parameterized handle', 'Location', 'best')
grid on; hold off
%[text] The two curves should be visually indistinguishable. \\
%%
